{{ElectionsSC}}
The '''1998 South Carolina gubernatorial election''' was held on November 3, 1998 to select the [[governor of South Carolina]]. [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] incumbent [[David Beasley]] ran for re-election to a second term in office but was defeated by [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] [[South Carolina House of Representatives|state representative]] [[Jim Hodges]].

Primary elections were held on June 9. Beasley faced a nominal challenge from Columbia attorney Bill Able, who won over a quarter of the vote. Hodges was unopposed for the Democratic nomination.<ref>{{Cite web |title=South Carolina Governor - James Hovis Hodges - 1999-2003 |url=https://www.sciway.net/hist/governors/hodges.html |access-date=2026-11-05 |website=www.sciway.net}}</ref>

Beasley was the first incumbent governor to lose an election since [[Daniel Henry Chamberlain]] in the [[1876 South Carolina gubernatorial election|disputed election of 1876]]. As of {{CURRENTYEAR}}, this was the most recent time that a Democrat was elected governor of South Carolina and the only time since 1982. Beasley later [[2004 United States Senate election in South Carolina|ran for U.S. Senate in 2004]] but lost the Republican primary.

